  "task": "Determine which specific rule (security group or network ACL) caused a VPC Flow Logs REJECT for a single TCP flow, and explain why no reverse-direction traffic appears in the logs.",
  "context": "User reports a VPC Flow Logs entry: single REJECT for TCP 10.0.1.5:51000 -> 10.0.2.9:3389 (RDP), with no reverse-direction flow logged at all (not even a reject). Need to identify the exact rule responsible for the block. Likely candidates: (a) destination instance's security group has no inbound rule allowing TCP 3389 from the source (SGs are default-deny, so absence of a matching allow rule causes implicit reject), (b) a Network ACL explicit DENY rule on the source or destination subnet for port 3389 or the ephemeral port range, (c) source instance's security group missing an outbound allow rule for TCP 3389 to the destination (though this would typically not appear as REJECT in flow logs the same way). Also determine why there's no reverse packet logged — this is expected behavior if the SG on the destination blocked it (no SYN-ACK ever sent back) or if a NACL deny stopped it inline before reaching the SG.",
  "resources": ["10.0.1.5 (source private IP)", "10.0.2.9 (destination private IP)", "account 012345678901_1", "region us-east-1 (assume VPC/subnets are in this region unless ENIs are found elsewhere)"],
  "timeframe": "current state of SG/NACL configuration (not time-bound)",
  "criteria": "1) Use EC2 describe_network_interfaces to find the ENIs owning private IPs 10.0.1.5 and 10.0.2.9, and note their VPC, subnet, and attached security groups. 2) Describe the security groups attached to the destination ENI (10.0.2.9) and check for inbound rules allowing TCP port 3389 from 10.0.1.5 or its security group/CIDR — identify if such a rule exists or is missing. 3) Describe the security groups attached to the source ENI (10.0.1.5) and check for outbound rules allowing TCP to port 3389. 4) Find the Network ACLs associated with both subnets and check inbound/outbound rules for TCP 3389 and the ephemeral port range (51000) for explicit DENY rules, noting rule numbers and CIDR ranges. 5) Conclude precisely which single rule (cite rule number/SG ID/NACL ID) is the most likely cause of the REJECT, and explain why the lack of a matching allow rule (or presence of an explicit deny) explains the one-way-only log entry."
}", "deep_research": false}, "type": "tool_use"}], "created_at": null}

